          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Iron deficiency anemia sits in the blood chapter. Which chapter would an underlying bleeding ulcer be coded from?",
          "answer": "**Iron deficiency anemia (D50):** Confirmed. The blood chapter (Chapter 3 of ICD-10-CM, D50 to D89) opens its nutritional anemia block with D50, iron-deficiency anemia. (Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 3: blood, blood-forming organs and the immune mechanism*.)\n\n**Bleeding peptic ulcer:** The digestive chapter (Chapter 11, K00 to K95) holds the esophagus, stomach and duodenum block at K20 to K31. Its peptic ulcer categories are divided by site: gastric, duodenal, peptic unspecified and gastrojejunal. (Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 11: diseases of the digestive system*.) So an ulcer is coded from Chapter 11, not from the blood chapter. My sources did not list the hemorrhage subcategories (K25 to K28) by name, so I can't confirm those specific codes from them.\n\n**The coding convention for anemia caused by a bleeding ulcer:** I could not answer this. My search tool failed, and the passages I retrieved don't address which code is sequenced first when an ulcer causes anemia. The \"with\" convention I found applies to diabetes and kidney disease, not to anemia. I won't fill this gap from memory, because the sequencing rules matter for accurate coding.\n\nTo get this answered, check the official ICD-10-CM Tabular List and Guidelines for Section I (conventions) and the anemia-due-to-blood-loss entries, or ask a certified medical coder. Coding decisions also depend on the full clinical record, which a general information tool can't review.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
